Ah, yes. If martial arts is what happens when knowledge becomes embodied, adaptive capability under contact with reality, then STEM has analogous “arts.” They just usually aren’t grouped under one name.

I’d call the general category Technical Arts or Applied Natural Arts:

\boxed{\text{Knowledge}+\text{Craft}+\text{Practice}+\text{Feedback}+\text{Flow}\rightarrow\text{Technical Art}}

The analogy isn’t STEM ↔ fighting. It’s more like:

\text{Science of combat}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{Martial Art}}

therefore:

\text{Science of }X\rightarrow\boxed{\text{Art of wielding }X}

Some existing fields already approximate this:

STEM knowledge	Its “martial art”
Physics	Engineering / experimental craft
Mathematics	Problem solving / mathematical modeling
Chemistry	Synthetic chemistry / laboratory craft
Biology	Medicine, bioengineering, fieldcraft
Computer science	Programming / software engineering
Electronics	Circuit design / hardware hacking
Mechanics	Machining / mechanical design
Materials science	Fabrication / metallurgy
Statistics	Inference / experimental design
Systems science	Systems engineering / operations research

But none quite captures what you’re asking, because martial arts integrates knowing + sensing + timing + improvising + embodiment + adversarial feedback.

A physicist knowing equations isn’t yet the equivalent of a fighter.

The equivalent is someone who looks at an unfamiliar physical system and almost feels:

load goes there; this will buckle; that’s the leverage point; change this geometry; conserve that; exploit this gradient.

That’s embodied physics.

Likewise, mathematical martial arts wouldn’t primarily mean knowing more theorems. It would mean recognizing the shape of an unfamiliar problem and fluidly deploying symmetry, decomposition, inversion, approximation, proof, transformation, dimensional reasoning, etc.

So build the STEM equivalent of martial arts

I’d call the whole thing:

\boxed{\textbf{Natural Arts}}

Not because they’re opposed to science. Because they are what happens when scientific knowledge becomes practiced capability.

Then you could have:

Mathematical Arts — wield relation, quantity, symmetry, proof and abstraction.

Physical Arts — wield force, energy, matter, geometry, motion and fields.

Chemical Arts — wield transformation, reaction, concentration, equilibrium and synthesis.

Biological Arts — wield adaptation, ecology, selection, growth and living systems.

Computational Arts — wield information, algorithms, representations, automation and machines.

Systems Arts — wield feedback, constraints, networks, incentives, emergence and control.

And training should resemble martial arts much more than a textbook curriculum.

You’d have forms: repeatedly execute canonical transformations until they’re automatic.

Sparring: receive unfamiliar problems where the correct technique isn’t announced.

Drills: isolate one primitive—symmetry, scaling, feedback, decomposition, estimation—and practice recognizing it across unrelated domains.

Flow: combine operators dynamically instead of following prescribed sequences.

Contact: predictions collide with experiments, code, measurements, prototypes, or reality.

After-action review: inspect why your chosen formation succeeded or failed.

Cross-training: solve the same problem as mathematician, engineer, programmer, biologist, designer.

And eventually no-form: stop thinking “which formula am I supposed to use?” and perceive the relational structure directly.

That is exactly what expertise often looks like.

Pantheon is already drifting toward this

We’ve been assembling a kind of semantic martial art.

The Indigo Hammer isn’t a fact. It’s a maneuver.

Bookends aren’t knowledge. They’re a maneuver.

Pull the Lever is a maneuver.

Symulation is a maneuver.

Perspective rotation is a maneuver.

The Metamap isn’t another technique; it’s increasingly the thing that learns which technique works against which terrain.

So the broader architecture could be:

\boxed{
\text{STEM}
=
\text{knowledge of mechanisms}
}

\boxed{
\text{Natural Arts}
=
\text{trained ability to wield mechanisms}
}

\boxed{
\text{Pantheon}
=
\text{meta-art for discovering, composing, transferring, testing, and training mechanisms}
}

And then something fun happens.

A martial artist doesn’t need to know beforehand exactly how the opponent will move. Training gives them a repertoire of primitives and transitions that can be composed under novel conditions.

That’s exactly what we’d want from STEM education:

Don’t train someone to remember the solution to yesterday’s problem.

Train them to recognize structure, muster mechanisms, make contact with reality, receive feedback, and flow into the next move.

That’s scientific knowledge becoming technical character.

Or in our language:

\boxed{\text{Know it}\rightarrow\text{practice it}\rightarrow\text{feel it}\rightarrow\text{wield it}\rightarrow\text{adapt it}}

Science discovers the moves. Art learns when and how to throw them.